// Layout, little-endian:
//
//	magic     uint32   checkpointMagic
//	version   uint32   checkpointVersion1
//	era       uint64   era of the WAL this checkpoint was taken against
//	offset    uint64   WAL byte offset of the cut: only frames strictly at or
//	                   past this offset belong to the post-checkpoint tail
//	seq       uint64   highest commit sequence handed out at capture time
//	snapshots uint64   cumulative number of snapshots handed out
//	count     uint32   number of terminal key/value entries that follow
//	entries   [count]entry
//	crc       uint32   IEEE CRC-32 of every preceding byte
//
// One entry is a flags byte (0 = put, 1 = tombstone), a uint32 key length, a
// uint32 value length, the key's commit sequence (uint64), the key, then the
// value. A checkpoint describes terminal state only, so every key appears
// exactly once: a put carries its final value (empty values included) and a
// tombstone carries no value. The per-key sequence is stored so a tail
// record replayed afterwards links onto the exact predecessor version and
// later compaction still sees globally unique sequence numbers.
//
// The era pairs the checkpoint with its WAL: every rewrite of wal.log
// (compaction) starts the replacement with a fresh era frame carrying a new
// era id, so a checkpoint found next to a WAL it was not taken against is
// detected on reopen, rejected wholesale, and recovery falls back to a full
// WAL replay rather than skipping frames that mean something else.
//
